{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Western North Carolina Railroad", "line": "Western North Carolina: Mainline", "id": "wncrr-salisbury-asheville", "color": "#BB3E03", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated for a 19th-century mountain-grade line with steep grades and sharp curves through the Blue Ridge; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1858, "end": 1861, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Chartered Feb 15 1855 as an extension of the North Carolina Railroad, to build from Salisbury toward the French Broad River beyond the Blue Ridge. Construction of the first 76-mile segment toward Morganton began in 1858; by 1859 the line had reached Morganton. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1861, "end": 1866, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Civil War virtually halted construction. Little progress was made until the state of North Carolina subscribed $4 million in 1866 to revive the project.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1866, "end": 1869, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "By 1868, financiers George W. Swepson and Gen. Milton S. Littlefield had gained control of the company amid a sprawling bond fraud scheme involving worthless securities and fraudulent stock subscriptions that helped bankrupt the state and led to Governor William W. Holden's 1871 impeachment. The state seized the railroad and purchased it outright for $665,000 in 1870.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1869, "end": 1879, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Eastern Division, 35.1 miles from Morganton to Old Fort, opened in 1869. Construction then stalled at the base of the Blue Ridge escarpment, the hardest engineering yet: 23.2 miles from Old Fort up to Azalea required seven tunnels to climb over 1,000 feet in nine miles. From 1877 the state, under Governor Zebulon Vance, supplied convict labor from the Central Prison to push the work through; the prison-camp population grew from just over 300 in 1875 to roughly 1,500 men (about 85% Black, many on trumped-up or minor charges), paid seven cents a day and housed in five camps along the grade.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1879, "end": 1880, "headway_min": 720, "note": "On March 11 1879 the 1,800-foot Swannanoa Tunnel was holed through - and that same day a cave-in, likely triggered by a runaway locomotive's brake failure during debris removal, killed at least 21 convict laborers and a guard. At least 125 prisoners are estimated to have died building this stretch alone, from cave-ins, disease, and guard violence; the dead were buried in unmarked pits near the tracks. The first train reached Asheville from Salisbury on October 3 1880, transforming the town from roughly 2,600 residents to over 10,000 within a decade.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1880, "end": 1894, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Operated as the state-owned Western North Carolina Railroad while its western extensions (see the Paint Rock and Murphy branches) were pushed through, also with convict labor, into the early 1890s. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1894, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Sold at foreclosure and conveyed to the Southern Railway on August 22 1894, becoming part of its Asheville-Salisbury main line alongside connections to Washington and the Piedmont. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}, {"start": 1971, "end": 1979, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Southern Railway continued to operate its own intercity passenger trains after Amtrak's May 1 1971 creation (Southern was one of the few railroads that opted out of joining Amtrak), including service over this line; frequency estimated, unsourced. Southern discontinued its last unsubsidized intercity passenger trains in 1979.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-80.47247, 35.67444], [-80.53065, 35.69037], [-80.54568, 35.69957], [-80.5513, 35.70193], [-80.55633, 35.70252], [-80.56243, 35.70651], [-80.57106, 35.70637], [-80.576